<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Predicting Estimated Times of Restoration for Electrical Outages Using Longitudinal Tabular Transformers</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote">This material is based upon work supported by Exelon.</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bogireddy Sai Prasanna Teja
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Valliappan Muthukaruppan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Carls Benjamin
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As climate variability increases, the ability of utility providers to deliver precise Estimated Times of Restoration (ETR) during natural disasters has become increasingly critical. Accurate and timely ETRs are essential for enabling customer preparedness during extended power outages, where informed decision-making can be crucial, particularly in severe weather conditions. Nonetheless, prevailing utility practices predominantly depend on manual assessments or traditional statistical methods, which often fail to achieve the level of precision required for reliable and actionable predictions. To address these limitations, we propose a Longitudinal Tabular Transformer (LTT) model that leverages historical outage event data along with sequential updates of these events to improve the accuracy of ETR predictions. The model’s performance was evaluated over 34,000 storm-related outage events from three major utility companies, collectively serving over 3 million customers over a 2-year period. Results demonstrate that the LTT model improves the Customer Satisfaction Impact (CSI) metric by an average of 19.08% (p&gt;0.001) compared to existing methods. Additionally, we introduce customer-informed regression metrics that align model evaluation with real-world satisfaction, ensuring the outcomes resonate with customer expectations. Furthermore, we employ interpretability techniques to analyze the temporal significance of incorporating sequential updates in modeling outage events and to identify the contributions of predictive features to a given ETR. This comprehensive approach not only improves predictive accuracy but also enhances transparency, fostering greater trust in the model’s capabilities.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Exelon</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">teja.reddy@exeloncorp.com, valliappan.muthukaruppan@exeloncorp.com, benjamin.carls@comed.com</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The increasing frequency and severity of natural calamities due to climate variability have led to a significant increase in power outages globally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Juan and Joan 2024</a>)</cite>. For utility companies, providing accurate Estimated Times of Restoration (ETRs) during such events is not merely a matter of customer service but a critical component of public safety. Timely and precise ETRs enable customers to make essential decisions, such as securing alternative power sources, preserving perishable goods, or evacuating vulnerable individuals. However, most utilities continue to rely on manual assessments or traditional statistical and machine learning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adibi and Milanicz 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wanik et al. 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walsh et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kar et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, which often lack the precision required to address the dynamic and evolving nature of outage data. This limitation frequently results in imprecise predictions, frustrating customers and hindering their preparedness during extended outages. A significant challenge in predicting ETRs arises from the dynamic nature of outage events, characterized by sequential updates as new information becomes available. These updates reflect evolving conditions, such as restoration progress, resource allocation, or corrections to earlier inaccuracies, thus framing the problem as a longitudinal regression task. Longitudinal modeling, which examines sequences of observations over time, is essential for refining predictions in response to ongoing developments. By capturing the temporal progression of outages, this approach leverages the complete event history, from initial conditions to subsequent updates, to provide increasingly accurate and refined predictions with each new update. Recent advancements in machine learning and deep learning present promising opportunities to improve the accuracy of ETR predictions in these dynamic settings. Transformer-based models, in particular, have demonstrated exceptional ability in capturing long-term dependencies in sequential data, making them highly suitable for regression tasks with longitudinal characteristics. By leveraging historical outage data along with the sequence of updates for each event, these models can reveal complex temporal patterns and interdependencies that traditional methods often overlook, thus enhancing predictive accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00225v1/storm-statistics.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:483/110;" width="483" height="110" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Storm-Realted Outage Counts between Jan 2022 to Jan 2024 for OPCO-1, OPCO-2, and OPCO-3.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a longitudinal transformer model specifically designed for the longitudinal tabular regression problem. We assess the model’s performance using outage data from three major utility companies, anonymized as OPCO-1, OPCO-2, and OPCO-3, collectively serving over 3 million customers. The anonymization ensures compliance with data privacy regulations and ethical standards, safeguarding sensitive customer information while enabling reproducible research. The evaluation encompasses various storm conditions classified as small, medium, and large to ensure the model’s robustness across different scenarios. To assess the practical utility of our predictions, we introduce novel regression metrics derived from our customer feedback surveys. Alongside traditional regression metrics like Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), we propose asymmetric regression metrics that reflect customer satisfaction levels more accurately. Customer feedback indicates that underprediction of ETRs significantly decreases satisfaction, as customers are unprepared for longer outages than anticipated. Conversely, overprediction of ETRs does not adversely affect customer satisfaction as long as the overestimation does not exceed eight hours; customers perceive this window as acceptable for planning purposes. However, overpredictions beyond eight hours again lead to a decline in satisfaction due to prolonged expectations of restoration. By incorporating this asymmetric tolerance into our evaluation metrics, we aim to align the model’s performance with real-world customer experiences and priorities. Additionally, we leverage attention matrix visualizations to analyze the temporal dependencies captured by our model, highlighting their critical role in enhancing predictive accuracy. To ensure interpretability, we employ techniques such as SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee 2017</a>)</cite> to quantify the contributions of individual features to ETR predictions. This analysis promotes transparency, builds stakeholder trust, and offers actionable insights to utility providers, supporting informed decision-making and development of effective communication strategies. The main contributions of our work are listed below:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a longitudinal tabular transformer model tailored for predicting ETRs. The model utilizes historical outage data and sequential updates to events to capture complex temporal patterns, updating ETR predictions dynamically as new information becomes available.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce novel regression evaluation metrics based on customer feedback to better represent the real-world impact of prediction accuracy on customer satisfaction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the role of temporal dependencies in enhancing predictive accuracy by visualizing attention matrices. Additionally, we apply interpretability techniques, such as SHAP, to identify key features influencing ETR predictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the model’s performance using anonymized outage data from three major utility companies serving 3 million customers, assessing its robustness across diverse storm conditions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, power outage data from January 2022 to January 2024 were analyzed across three operating companies, collectively serving a customer base of 3 million across distinct geographic regions. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ Predicting Estimated Times of Restoration for Electrical Outages Using Longitudinal Tabular Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, multiple significant storm events of varying intensities occurred during the observation period, leading to extensive power disruptions and numerous outage incidents. Each outage event underwent multiple updates during restoration as more accurate information became available, reflecting continuous revisions and corrections to initial reports. The analysis focused on 34,000 storm-related outage events with over 200,000 updates, each update encompassing 92 features derived from outage-related variables such as the time of the outage, the most recent update time, the number of customers affected, crew dispatch status, the number of concurrent events, and the average restoration time for recently resolved events. To construct the training, validation, and testing datasets for each operating company (OPCO), storm statistics were incorporated, and a stratified sampling approach was employed, based on storm magnitude. The validation and test datasets were deliberately designed to include at least two storms from each magnitude category—small, medium, and large—ensuring balanced representation across varying storm intensities. The storms were classified as large, medium, or small by normalizing the total number of customers affected by a storm relative to the total number of customers served within each OPCO.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Baseline Comparisons</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess the performance of our Longitudinal Tabular Transformer by comparing it with eight established machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) methods commonly used for tabular data. For the ML models, we include four standard and computationally efficient baselines: Linear Regression (LR), along with three prominent tree-based boosting algorithms: XGBoost (XGB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin 2016</a>)</cite>, LightGBM (LGBM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. 2017</a>)</cite>, and CatBoost (CB) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prokhorenkova et al. 2018</a>)</cite>. To facilitate comparisons with more complex architectures, we further incorporate four DL models: Fully Connected Network (FCN), ResNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2016</a>)</cite>, TabTransformer (TT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2020</a>)</cite>, and FT-Transformer (FTT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gorishniy et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. While preprocessing the data for ML models, we applied feature standardization as appropriate. Missing values in continuous variables were imputed using mean imputation, while categorical variables were imputed using mode imputation. Categorical features were subsequently processed using one-hot encoding. For the DL models, continuous features underwent Z-normalization. In the FCN and ResNet architectures, categorical variables were processed via one-hot encoding. For the TabTransformer and FT-Transformer models, categorical variables were label-encoded before being passed to the embedding layers. These preprocessing steps ensure consistency across models, enabling a robust performance comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Asymmetric Loss Function for Customer Satisfaction</h3>

<figure id="Sx2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00225v1/csat.png" id="Sx2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:354/157;" width="354" height="157" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Storm-Outage Related Customer Satisfaction Survey Analysis</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of outage restoration, accurately predicting the ETR is critical for maintaining customer satisfaction. However, insights from our internal customer outage survey analysis as illustrated in Figure <a href="#Sx2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Asymmetric Loss Function for Customer Satisfaction ‣ Experimental Setup ‣ Predicting Estimated Times of Restoration for Electrical Outages Using Longitudinal Tabular Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> have shown that customer sentiment exhibits a strong asymmetric behavior with respect to prediction errors in ETR. Specifically, under-predictions where the actual restoration time exceeds the predicted ETR significantly diminish customer satisfaction. Conversely, minor over-predictions (up to 8 hours) have little impact on customer sentiment, while over-predictions greater than 8 hours once again lead to a noticeable decrease in customer satisfaction. Due to this asymmetric impact on customer satisfaction, traditional loss functions like Mean Squared Error (MSE) inadequately capture the varying consequences of prediction errors on customer experiences. To address this issue, we propose an asymmetric loss function that penalizes under-prediction more heavily than over-prediction, and penalizes large over-predictions more than small ones. This design aligns the model’s loss function with customer satisfaction dynamics, ensuring that prediction errors are penalized based on their real-world impact.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Formulation of the Asymmetric Loss Function</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the true ETR for event <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predicted ETR for the same event. The prediction error <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the difference between the predicted and true ETR:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{i}=\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{i}=\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The asymmetric loss function <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\hat{y},y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(\epsilon_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\hat{y},y)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(\epsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\epsilon_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\epsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a piecewise loss function that applies different penalties depending on the nature of the error:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\epsilon_{i})=\begin{cases}\alpha\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&lt;0\quad\text{(under-prediction)}\\
|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }0\leq\epsilon_{i}\leq 8\quad\text{(over-prediction within 8 hrs)}\\
\beta\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&gt;8\quad\text{(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(under-prediction)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(over-prediction within 8 hrs)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\epsilon_{i})=\begin{cases}\alpha\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&lt;0\quad\text{(under-prediction)}\\
|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }0\leq\epsilon_{i}\leq 8\quad\text{(over-prediction within 8 hrs)}\\
\beta\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&gt;8\quad\text{(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.SSSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this formulation:</p>
<ul id="Sx2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> controls the severity of the penalty for under-prediction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Sx2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> controls the penalty for over-prediction when the error exceeds 8 hours.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of the proposed model and facilitate a comparison with baseline models, we define a set of metrics that closely align with customer satisfaction, in addition to the traditional Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). These metrics are designed to assess the model’s ability to effectively balance under-prediction and over-prediction, with a specific focus on mitigating the impact of large prediction errors. This approach is informed by insights derived from our customer survey analysis, ensuring that the evaluation framework captures the nuances of customer expectations.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Under-prediction Rate (UPR)</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the significant dissatisfaction caused by under-prediction, we define the Under-prediction Rate (UPR) as the proportion of predictions where the predicted ETR <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is less than the actual ETR <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This metric captures the frequency of under-predictions, which is crucial for evaluating customer sentiment:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UPR}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}(\hat{y}_{i}&lt;y_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>UPR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UPR}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}(\hat{y}_{i}&lt;y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function that returns 1 if the condition is true and 0 otherwise. A lower UPR indicates fewer under-predictions, which aligns with improved customer satisfaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Over-prediction Beyond 8 Hours Rate (OPR-8)</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure the impact of large over-predictions, we define the Over-prediction Beyond 8 Hours Rate (OPR-8) as the proportion of predictions where the predicted ETR exceeds the actual ETR by more than 8 hours. This metric is important because over-predictions beyond 8 hours have been shown to negatively affect customer satisfaction:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{OPR-8}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}&gt;8)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>OPR-8</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{OPR-8}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbb{I}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i}&gt;8)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A lower OPR-8 reflects fewer large over-predictions, which helps maintain higher levels of customer satisfaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Weighted Asymmetric Error (WAE)</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the above metrics, we introduce the Weighted Asymmetric Error (WAE), a customer-centric metric that combines under- and over-prediction penalties in a single measure. This metric is derived from the asymmetric loss function and penalizes under-predictions more heavily than over-predictions, with additional penalties for over-predictions exceeding 8 hours. It is defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{WAE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(\epsilon_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>WAE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{WAE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(\epsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\epsilon_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\epsilon_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the same piecewise loss function used in the asymmetric loss function:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\epsilon_{i})=\begin{cases}\alpha\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&lt;0\quad\text{(under-prediction)}\\
|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }0\leq\epsilon_{i}\leq 8\quad\text{(over-prediction within 8 hrs)}\\
\beta\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&gt;8\quad\text{(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(under-prediction)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(over-prediction within 8 hrs)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\epsilon_{i})=\begin{cases}\alpha\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&lt;0\quad\text{(under-prediction)}\\
|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }0\leq\epsilon_{i}\leq 8\quad\text{(over-prediction within 8 hrs)}\\
\beta\cdot|\epsilon_{i}|,&amp;\text{if }\epsilon_{i}&gt;8\quad\text{(over-prediction beyond 8 hrs)}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This metric provides a weighted measure of model performance, with explicit penalties reflecting the real-world consequences of prediction errors on customer satisfaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Customer Satisfaction Impact (CSI)</h4>

<div id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, to provide a direct estimate of customer satisfaction based on model performance, we define a Customer Satisfaction Impact (CSI) that combines the penalties from under- and over-predictions into a single interpretable score. The CSI is defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CSI}=1-\left(\alpha\cdot\text{UPR}+\beta\cdot\text{OPR-8}\right)/(\alpha+\beta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CSI</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>UPR</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>OPR-8</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CSI}=1-\left(\alpha\cdot\text{UPR}+\beta\cdot\text{OPR-8}\right)/(\alpha+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx2.SSx4.SSSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are derived from industrial research on customer satisfaction with expired and over-predicted ETRs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geno 2023</a>)</cite>. A higher CSI indicates a higher level of customer satisfaction, with perfect customer satisfaction corresponding to a score of 1. This score allows for a more intuitive comparison between models with respect to their impact on customer satisfaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Longitudinal Tabular Transformer</h2>

<figure id="Sx3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00225v1/architecture.png" id="Sx3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:491/197;" width="491" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Architecture of Longitudinal Tabular Transformer</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we address the challenge of predicting the ETR for outage events by leveraging longitudinal data. Each outage event is characterized by a series of sequential updates over time, with each update providing the latest information on both categorical and continuous features relevant to the event’s evolving status. By framing the task as a longitudinal problem, we aim to capture the dynamic temporal patterns and dependencies that emerge as new information becomes available in subsequent updates. This approach enables a more effective utilization of temporal data by modeling the evolution of an event’s status, thereby improving prediction accuracy. To effectively capture these temporal dependencies and feature-based interactions, we propose a transformer-based model that represents each event as a sequence of updates, where each update corresponds to the latest outage information at a given timestep. This representation allows us to leverage the self-attention mechanism inherent in transformer models, facilitating the modeling of complex interactions between successive updates and their associated features. By explicitly incorporating temporal sequences into the predictive framework, our approach enhances the ability to detect patterns and relationships across updates and features, which would be less effective in static or single-point-in-time models. Below, we formalize the problem and describe the construction of our model:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Setup</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}=\{e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}=\{e_{1},e_{2},\dots,e_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a set of <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> outage events. Each event <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of a sequence of updates over time, such that:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=\{r_{i,1},r_{i,2},\dots,r_{i,M_{i}}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=\{r_{i,1},r_{i,2},\dots,r_{i,M_{i}}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of updates for event <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th update for event <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,2,\dots,M_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,2,\dots,M_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each update provides the latest information about the outage event. Given this sequence of updates, the goal is to predict a target variable <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as the Estimated Time to Restoration (ETR), for event <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> based on all updates up to the current time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Feature Representation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each update <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe two types of features: <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">categorical</span> and <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">continuous</span> features, as well as a temporal feature represented by the timestamp of the update. As part of the data preprocessing, all continuous features were Z-normalized, while categorical features were label-encoded before being passed to the embedding layer.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Categorical Features</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{i,j}=\{C^{1}_{i,j},C^{2}_{i,j},\dots,C^{p}_{i,j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{i,j}=\{C^{1}_{i,j},C^{2}_{i,j},\dots,C^{p}_{i,j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{k}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{k}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the value of the <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th categorical feature at update <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for event <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of categorical features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Continuous Features</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i,j}=\{X^{1}_{i,j},X^{2}_{i,j},\dots,X^{q}_{i,j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i,j}=\{X^{1}_{i,j},X^{2}_{i,j},\dots,X^{q}_{i,j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{k}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{k}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the value of the <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th continuous feature at update <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for event <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of continuous features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Temporal Features</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each update <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs at a specific time <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The temporal progression between updates is captured by the time delta, defined as:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{i,j}=t_{i,j}-t_{i,1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{i,j}=t_{i,j}-t_{i,1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the timestamp of the first update for the event <math id="Sx3.SSx2.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Model Input Construction</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We transform the categorical features into continuous embeddings for use in the transformer model. For each categorical feature <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{k}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{k}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define an embedding function <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Embedding}(C^{k}_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Embedding</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Embedding}(C^{k}_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math> that maps the categorical value to a vector in <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of the embedding space for feature <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The concatenated embedding vector for all categorical features at update <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> of event <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i,j}=\left[\text{Embedding}(C^{1}_{i,j}),\dots,\text{Embedding}(C^{p}_{i,j})\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{\sum_{k=1}^{p}d_{k}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Embedding</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>Embedding</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i,j}=\left[\text{Embedding}(C^{1}_{i,j}),\dots,\text{Embedding}(C^{p}_{i,j})\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{\sum_{k=1}^{p}d_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The continuous features <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are concatenated with the embedded categorical features <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the resulting vector is passed through a linear projection layer <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LP</annotation></semantics></math> to form the feature vector <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> for update <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Sx3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i,j}=LP\left(\left[\mathbf{e}_{i,j};\mathbf{X}_{i,j}\right]\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i,j}=LP\left(\left[\mathbf{e}_{i,j};\mathbf{X}_{i,j}\right]\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To incorporate temporal information, we use the time delta <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta t_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta t_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute a positional encoding. Specifically, we define a positional encoding function <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PE}(\Delta t_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>PE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PE}(\Delta t_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math> that maps the time delta to a vector in <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of the model’s hidden representation:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{pe}_{i,j}=\text{PE}(\Delta t_{i,j})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>PE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{pe}_{i,j}=\text{PE}(\Delta t_{i,j})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The final input representation for update <math id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of the feature vector and the positional encoding:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i,j}=\mathbf{z}_{i,j}+\mathbf{pe}_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐩𝐞</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i,j}=\mathbf{z}_{i,j}+\mathbf{pe}_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Transformer Encoding</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each event <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence of updates up to the <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th update forms the input sequence:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i}=\left[\mathbf{h}_{i,1},\mathbf{h}_{i,2},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{i,j}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{j\times d_{\text{model}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i}=\left[\mathbf{h}_{i,1},\mathbf{h}_{i,2},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{i,j}\right]\in\mathbb{R}^{j\times d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This sequence is passed through L layers of transformer encoders, which are designed to capture dependencies across the sequence of updates. Each transformer encoder consists of multiple layers of multi-head self-attention and feedforward networks. Let <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the output of the <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, with the initial input being <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(0)}=\mathbf{H}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(0)}=\mathbf{H}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sequence of updates across layers is given by:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l+1)}=\text{TransformerLayer}(\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l)},\text{Mask}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TransformerLayer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>Mask</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l+1)}=\text{TransformerLayer}(\mathbf{H}_{i}^{(l)},\text{Mask}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Mask}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Mask</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Mask}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a padding mask to handle sequences of variable length. The final output from the transformer encoder is the sequence of update-level representations:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i}^{\text{final}}=\left[\mathbf{h}_{i,1}^{\text{final}},\mathbf{h}_{i,2}^{\text{final}},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i}^{\text{final}}=\left[\mathbf{h}_{i,1}^{\text{final}},\mathbf{h}_{i,2}^{\text{final}},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Target Prediction</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We predict the target variable <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for event <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Sx3.SSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th update using the below approach:</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Last Update Representation</h4>

<div id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the representation corresponding to the last update from the transformer block, <math id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as an input to an FCN layer to make the prediction:</p>
<table id="Sx3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}=\text{FCN}(\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>FCN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mtext>final</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}=\text{FCN}(\mathbf{h}_{i,j}^{\text{final}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <span id="Sx3.SSx5.SSSx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">FCN</span> is a fully connected network that maps the update representation to the target.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Architecture and Hyperparameters</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the proposed LTT model, the max sequence length was 20, embedding dimension was set to 128, and 6 transformer encoder layers were used, each with 16 self-attention heads. Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kinga, Adam et al. 2015</a>)</cite> with a starting learning rate of 0.0001 and a batch size of 1024 was used. To improve convergence, the learning rate was reduced by a factor of 0.7 every time the validation metric plateaued. For a fair comparison, machine learning baseline models were tuned via grid search to determine their optimal hyperparameters. Similarly, deep learning baseline models were configured with training settings aligned with those used for the LTT model, ensuring all models were evaluated at their maximum potential. In the asymmetric loss, <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> parameters are critical for calibrating the model to the specific dynamics of customer satisfaction. To determine appropriate values for these parameters, we leveraged insights from research conducted by JD Power <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geno 2023</a>)</cite> on the relative proportions of customer dissatisfaction associated with UPR and OPR-8. The values for <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx3.SSx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are set to 5 and 2, respectively. All deep learning models were implemented using PyTorch.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Results and Discussion</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a comparative analysis of the proposed Longitudinal Tabular Transformer (LTT) model against a wide range of established models for tabular regression tasks. The evaluation encompasses both popular deep learning approaches for tabular regression as well as widely used machine learning models known for their effectiveness with tabular data. For the baseline models, the task was treated as a conventional tabular regression problem, adhering to the constraints of these models, which are designed to process only static, one-dimensional data. In contrast, the LTT model reinterprets the task as a longitudinal tabular problem, enabling it to capture temporal dependencies and effectively manage multi-dimensional data within a tabular framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00225v1/attention.png" id="Sx4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/236;" width="334" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Attention Weights Heatmap for OPCO-1 LTT Model</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experimental Results</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the comparative analysis, we train and evaluate separate Longitudinal Tabular Transformer (LTT) and baseline models for each OPCO. The proposed LTT model demonstrates superior predictive performance across diverse storm intensities, outperforming most of the traditional and deep learning baseline models in estimating time to restoration (ETR). Table 1 provides a summary of the model’s performance, averaged over five trials, across five metrics: UPR, OPR-8, WAE, CSI, and RMSE. These metrics are evaluated across storms categorized by intensity (large, medium, and small) for three utility companies: OPCO-1, OPCO-2, and OPCO-3. The LTT model consistently demonstrates lower UPR and OPR-8 values, indicating reduced occurrences of both significant under-predictions and over-predictions compared to baseline models. Notably, the LTT model’s weighted asymmetric error (WAE) remains the lowest among all tested models across various storm intensities and utility companies, underscoring its robustness and adaptability to events of differing magnitudes. Furthermore, the LTT model achieves the highest CSI values across all storm sizes, reflecting strong alignment with customer satisfaction expectations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">Large Storms (L)</span>: In large storm scenarios, the LTT model demonstrated a substantial reduction in Weighted Asymmetric Error (WAE) compared to all baseline models, including conventional tree-based methods like XGBoost (XGB) and LightGBM (LGBM). It also achieved notably lower Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) scores. These results underscore the model’s predictive accuracy and robustness even in high-impact situations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808000;">Medium Storms (M)</span>: For medium-intensity storms, the LTT model consistently outperformed advanced deep learning models, such as ResNet and TabTransformer (TT), in both WAE and RMSE metrics. It also achieved notably lower Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) scores, particularly for OPCO-1 and OPCO-3. This sustained performance across moderate storm scenarios highlights the model’s effectiveness in managing outage events of this severity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Small Storms (S)</span>: During less intense storm events, the LTT model exhibited the lowest Under-Prediction Rate (UPR) and Over-Prediction Rate within eight hours (OPR-8), aligning closely with customer expectations by minimizing both under- and over-predictions. It also achieved notably lower Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) scores, particularly for OPCO-1 and OPCO-3. The model’s high CSI values across various operational companies (OPCO-1, OPCO-2, and OPCO-3) further confirm its customer-oriented predictive accuracy for smaller, less disruptive events.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis highlights the proposed LTT model’s ability to dynamically incorporate sequential data and consistently outperform traditional tabular approaches across the metrics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Metric</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">OPCO-1</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text">OPCO-2</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text">OPCO-3</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx4.T1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">L</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808000;">M</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">S</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">L</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808000;">M</span></td>
<td id="Sx4.T1.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="Sx4.T1.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">S</span></td>
