<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The mean UPR, OPR-8, WAE, CSI, and RMSE values are reported for the proposed Longitudinal Tabular Transformer (LTT) and other baseline models. Results are averaged over five trials across storm sizes categorized as <span id="Sx4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF0040;">Large (L)</span>, <span id="Sx4.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808000;">Medium (M)</span>, and <span id="Sx4.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Small (S)</span> for the OPCO-1, OPCO-2, and OPCO-3 utilities. The best-performing methods, based on the WAE metric, are highlighted in color. The arrows <math id="Sx4.T1.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{0,1,0}\uparrow}(high)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;" mathcolor="#00FF00" stretchy="false">↑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{0,1,0}\uparrow}(high)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Sx4.T1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\downarrow}(low)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000" stretchy="false">↓</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\color[rgb]{1,0,0}\downarrow}(low)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the expected directional trends of the metric.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Analysis of Attention Layers</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To gain a comprehensive understanding of the temporal dynamics captured by our model, we conduct an analysis of the model’s attention layers. The self-attention mechanism within the transformer model allows for differential assignment of importance to various updates within the sequence, thus capturing dependencies over time. By visualizing attention weights, we can observe the model’s prioritization of information across different updates, yielding valuable insights into the temporal impact of each update on the final ETR prediction.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Attention Visualization Methodology</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To visualize the attention weights:</p>
<ol id="Sx4.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by extracting attention weights from randomly selected attention heads within each transformer layer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the average attention weight assigned by the model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These average weights are subsequently visualized as a heatmap, where the x-axis represents the query update’s position within the sequence, and the y-axis denotes the key update’s position within the sequence.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Interpretation of Attention Weights</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Figure <a href="#Sx4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Results and Discussion ‣ Predicting Estimated Times of Restoration for Electrical Outages Using Longitudinal Tabular Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which illustrates the attention weights of six layers from the transformer model for OPCO-1, we observe distinct patterns in how attention is allocated across different layers and query-key positions. Each subplot represents the attention weights for a specific layer, with the x-axis denoting key positions and the y-axis representing query positions. The color gradient, ranging from dark purple (low attention weight) to bright yellow (high attention weight), illustrates the intensity of attention assigned at each position. Key observations include:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer-Specific Attention Patterns</span>: The attention patterns vary considerably across layers. For instance, Layers 1, 5 and 6 show a more dispersed attention pattern across query-key positions, suggesting that these layers capture broader dependencies across the sequence. In contrast, Layers 2, 3, and 4 demonstrate higher concentration in specific positions, indicated by the prominent yellow bands in certain regions, suggesting that these layers may focus on particular points in the sequence that are more informative for the model’s prediction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Focus on Early Revisions</span>: Layers 3, 4, 5, and 6 assign higher attention weights to early query positions, indicating that initial updates provide critical baseline information. For example, early updates about outage severity or crew deployment may serve as a foundation for subsequent predictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer 2 and Critical Temporal Dependencies</span>: The diagonal band in Layer 2 highlights the model’s focus on temporal continuity, ensuring that updates in close temporal proximity are strongly connected. This helps the model track how events evolve over time.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contextual Integration in Later Layers</span>: Layers 5 and 6 exhibit more evenly distributed attention, reflecting broader contextual integration by consolidating insights from both early critical updates and later refinements, while consistently assigning low attention to rightmost positions, which often correspond to padding sequences.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2505.00225v1/SHAP.png" id="Sx4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:334/236;" width="334" height="236" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Top 5 features identified through SHAP analysis across the first five event revisions for OPCO-3, highlighting the evolving impact of key features on the predicted Estimated Time of Restoration (ETR).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Feature Importance Analysis Using SHAP</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further interpret the model, we employ SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee 2017</a>)</cite> values, a game-theoretic approach that assigns contribution values to individual features, to quantify their influence on the model’s ETR predictions across various updates. This method provides a consistent framework for understanding the contribution of each feature to the model’s outputs.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">SHAP Analysis Methodology</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The methodology to generate SHAP values consists of the following steps:</p>
<ol id="Sx4.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="Sx4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A representative subset of the dataset is selected, and SHAP values are calculated to quantify the contribution of each feature to the model’s predictions for individual samples within the subset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SHAP values are aggregated using absolute mean to derive the overall importance of each feature.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The top five most significant features, as determined by their aggregated SHAP importance scores, are visualized across first five revisions to illustrate how their influence on predictions evolves over time.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Interpretation of SHAP Values</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The SHAP analysis (Figure <a href="#Sx4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Interpretation of Attention Weights ‣ Analysis of Attention Layers ‣ Results and Discussion ‣ Predicting Estimated Times of Restoration for Electrical Outages Using Longitudinal Tabular Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) highlights the top five key features influencing OPCO-3’s LLT model predictions during the first five event revisions. Our key findings are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx4.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx4.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Most Important Features</span>: Features such as ”Number of Customers Under Outage”, and ”Number of Events Where Crew is Dispatched” consistently appear across different revisions. These features highlight the importance of immediate customer impact and operational response timing, as they are crucial drivers in estimating the restoration time accurately.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Temporal Changes</span>: The influence of specific features evolves as revisions progress. For instance, in earlier revisions, features like ”Number of Customers Under Outage” and ”Priority” are prominent, reflecting the model’s emphasis on immediate priority assessments and customer impact at the onset of the outage. In later revisions, features such as ”Number of Events Where Crew Is Dispatched” and ”Rolling Average of Restore Time for Last 25 Closed Events” gain prominence, indicating a shift towards understanding cumulative restoration patterns and recent crew activities as more data becomes available over time.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Operational and Situational Dynamics</span>: Features that track crew assignment status, such as ”Number of Times an Event Status Changed to Crew Dispatched” and ”Number of Times an Event Status Changed to Crew Unassigned,” play a crucial role in middle to later revisions. This trend suggests the model adapts its predictions based on real-time changes in crew availability and assignment dynamics, underscoring the importance of responsive resource management in influencing ETR predictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx4.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx4.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.I7.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consistency Across Revisions</span>: Certain features, such as ”Number of Customers Under Outage” and ”Number of Events Where Crew Completed Restoration,” consistently appear across multiple revisions. This consistency highlights that both contextual factors (e.g., whether the outage is large or small) and recent operational outcomes (e.g., recent completions) are essential components that provide consistent predictive power for ETRs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these insights validate the model’s alignment with customer satisfaction priorities and offer transparency into its decision-making process. This interpretation framework serves as a robust tool for evaluating and explaining model predictions in outage restoration scenarios, allowing for a nuanced understanding of features as situational dynamics shift.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a Longitudinal Tabular Transformer (LTT) model that marks a significant advancement in predicting ETRs during power outages. This model integrates dynamic predictive capabilities with customer-focused metrics to enhance both accuracy and user satisfaction. The primary contributions of this study include the development of a transformer-based architecture that updates ETR predictions in real time by leveraging sequential event data, thereby improving its practical utility for service providers. The model’s interpretability features, such as SHAP analysis, enhance transparency and build trust among stakeholders by identifying the most critical factors influencing predictions. With minor architectural modifications, the LTT model can accommodate a wider variety of features, such as static inputs, dynamic variables, and features of varying lengths, demonstrating its adaptability. Furthermore, this architecture can be seamlessly extended to classification tasks and other predictive applications, broadening its utility beyond outage prediction. The potential of the LTT model extends to addressing other longitudinal challenges across diverse domains that require sequential data modeling. This underscores a promising direction for future research and the broader application.</p>
</div>
</section>
